these are wigar workers featured on  chinese state media  celebrating the islamic festival of eid  al-adha  they are not in xinjiang their home but  thousands of miles away  in shandong east china rights groups  allege that sending uighurs to factories  around the country is indicative of  forced  labor that they are subject to strict  controls as part of a policy of  assimilation  china rejects those claims but sky news  has uncovered evidence of the tight  social and political controls applied to  weak workers  on the chinese internet there are  hundreds of postings offering uighur  labor  in batches of 50 to 100 people this one  says that the security of workers will  be guaranteed by the government  the company hosting the adverts did not  respond to a request for comment  we phoned some of the numbers listed  [Music]  ultimately the government decides where  workers go another agent told us  when they get that government approval  the workers end up in factories like the  one in the video  we went to shandong to find out more  when we arrived at the seafood  processing factory  it was a very different scene there are  no uyghurs here  the factory owner told us they've all  returned home because of the pandemic  he described the idea of forced labor as  nonsense saying workers came here  because they chose to  earned at least 300 pounds per month and  had air conditioners in their on-site  dormitories  but they were under close supervision  the cctv cameras in the office monitor  those dormitories  riot control gear is also at hand and if  workers wish to leave the factory to go  shopping the owner told us  the company would drive them into buses  in the meantime he had called the police  12 officers and communist party  officials questioned us for two hours  before ordering us to leave town our  questions were not welcome at another  seafood factory in the same province  for years it has run a uk subsidiary  registered in london  this manager was waiting at the door he  told us bluntly there were no  wiga workers here nor had there been in  the several years he had been employed  at the factory  that's contradicted by photos posted in  march last year on the company's website  which have since been deleted it shows  uyga workers arriving from xinjiang  the accompanying announcement says that  their arrival will help poverty  alleviation  and the integration of the national  family this is a coastal  export city some two thousand miles away  from landlocked xinjiang it comes with a  very different  way of life uighur workers are being  sent all across  china to different cities at the very  least it is a  vast labor transfer program and it's one  that requires a whole industry  around it the chinese government has  been mounting an aggressive publicity  campaign  in reaction to the allegations of forced  labour  we were invited to this press conference  in beijing earlier this year  questions had to be pre-submitted in  order to attend  we were told this uyghur man had taken  part in the labor transfer program  if this labor isn't worth if it is  enforced why do they need government  supervision  [Music]  sky news asked the chinese government  for a response to our story  but after a week had received no reply  even here in east china so far away from  xinjiang  answers are hard to find
 

